A||||6||BAM-BAM||There Must Be An Easier Way!!!!||||8||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||03-11-2000||11:16 PM||||   C'mon now, I have read post's where you guys were taking your EGR tubes on and off several times in a day. I just change mine;  <BR>"WHAT A ***** " <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> There has got to be something wrong in my methodic process. It was the hardest thing I have had to do on this car yet. I disconnected the battery, Removed the distributor cap, Covered that, then I embarked on the worst two and a half hour quest of my life <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> Is there a special offset wrench that is used. It would have made it alot simpler. I didn't have one so, it was a quarter turn at a  time. Also is there a front and back to the EGR tube gaskets?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> Sorry, needed to vent. <P>  Tanx Alot,<BR>  BAM-MAD||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||03-11-2000||11:30 PM||||When I replaced my EGR tube, I spent forever trying to get the bolts to thread in. I finally gave up and pulled the plenum off. Would have saved a lot of time if I had just donw that in the first place. I do remember one gasket is directional, don't remember which one though. It was the the gasked with the little steel disk in the center.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||03-11-2000||11:45 PM||||you all work to hard and don't watch enought New Yankee Workshop... go to Sears get a set of Craftsman Gear Wrenchs..  I have a set and can WAIL on them with a hammer and they haven't broken!.. worth every cent of the $30. You can change this tube take off tranny mounts and just about everything is accessable with them.  Snap-op and Matco both have their versions of them but their all about the same... just priced different. Must have for the Fiero fixer...  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||cobra427||03-12-2000||12:48 AM||||when I took my EGR tube off,I used the 10mm wrench to breakloose the bolts or nuts( I don't remember which, I think that the nuts were on the valve and the bolts were on the plenum)and then I used a flexible shaft (1/4" drive)with the 10mm socket to unscrew the bolt?-nut? the rest of the way.<BR>The tool is like a nut driver except that it has a flexible shaft.<BR>To replace the tube, I found that if you started with the left side plenum bolt,it seems to make things go a little easier. Be sure to have a magnetic shaft handy just in case you drop the bolt. IMHO, it was made a little easier because I replaced it with the IRM flexible EGR tube. Made life a lot easier.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||03-12-2000||12:51 AM||||  It took a little perseverance, but I got it done <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> Now, about that gasket with the little steel disc, there wasn't one in the set that I got. However the instructions indicated that there should  be one. There also was no steel disc on the one that came off. It was supposed to be at the plenum end and marked on the tube side <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> I believe I shall invest in those,afore mentioned gear wrenches. Like I said there had to be a better way. One more question how do you effectively get a torque wrench on there?<P>  Thanx Again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>   BAM-BAM||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||03-12-2000||08:57 AM||||I didn't use a torque wrench when I replaced the tube. Just snugged the bolts down until they felt tight.||||reg||1
Z||000006||wkayl||03-12-2000||09:32 AM||||As my uncle used to say, the correct torque is to "turn them till they twist off, then back off 1/2 turn."||||reg||12
